.TH CBMAP_INSERT_ELEM 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_insert_elem \- insert a key-value pair into the BST map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

ccol_retval_t cbmap_insert_elem(cbmap cbm,
                                 const cmap_pair *key_pair,
                                 const cmap_pair *val_pair);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Inserts a key-value pair into the BST map, or replaces the old value when
the key is already there, and then rebalances the tree with AVL
rotations.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cbm        BST map to insert into
key_pair   Key to insert (the function copies it into the map)
val_pair   Value for that key (the function copies it into the map)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success               - A new key was inserted
ccol_key_already_present   - The key was already in the map, and its
                             value was updated
ccol_container_full        - The map holds ccol_max_elem_count elements
ccol_not_enough_memory     - A memory allocation failed
ccol_invalid_args          - key_pair or val_pair is NULL, one of the
                             two pairs carries a NULL ptr with a
                             non-zero size, the size of key_pair
                             doesn't match the size of a fixed-width
                             key type (see cbmap_create_full(3)), or
                             the map has ccol_string keys and the
                             size of key_pair is 0
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The ptr of a key_pair can have any alignment, because the comparison
reads the key bytes with memcpy and never through a pointer cast to the
key type. key_pair->ptr may point straight at key bytes at an arbitrary
offset in a larger buffer, such as a field sliced out of a packed network
frame or a memory-mapped record, with no copy into an aligned local
first.

The insert is O(log n), and the key data and the value data are copied
into the map. When the key is already present, the old value is freed and
the new one takes its place. The function asserts if cbm is NULL.

A NULL key_pair or val_pair is reported as ccol_invalid_args, as
chmap_insert_elem(3) does. A pair whose ptr is NULL and whose size is not
zero promises bytes it has no storage for, so it is ccol_invalid_args too.

A size of 0 is valid for the value, and for the key of a map with
ccol_other_types keys, with or without a ptr, and stores an empty value or
an empty key. A string key (ccol_string) counts its terminating NUL, so
its size is at least 1, and a key_pair of size 0 on such a map is
ccol_invalid_args, as in chmap_insert_elem(3). chmap_insert_elem(3) also
refuses a value, and a key of any type, of size zero.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_insert (3),
.BR cbmap_delete_elem (3),
.BR cbmap_get_elem_ref (3)
